Reading: incarceration's wealth penalty is large and highly significant at every tier, but MODERATION by baseline resources is significant ONLY on the dollar scale. Because the dollar scale is arithmetic, a roughly constant proportional penalty mechanically produces a bigger dollar gap higher up. The tier gradient is therefore reported as a DOLLAR-SCALE finding, and as descriptive (not estimated moderation) on the relative scale.

## 5. Baseline imbalance / level vs change framing
pooled: 1985 gap -4,853 [CI -8,342, -1,103]; 1996 level gap -44,380; 1985->1996 change gap -39,526 [CI -52,559, -27,621].
=> only ~11% of the 1996 gap was present at baseline; ~89% accumulated over the window. Within tiers: baseline gap T1 +153, T2 -277, T3 -9,894 [CI -20,172, +1,566]; the T3 baseline difference is NOT distinguishable from zero, so the T3 1996 gap of -86k is predominantly post-baseline divergence, not pre-existing gap. Level and change framings agree.

## 6. Ratio trajectory (inflation-robust view)
Black males pooled ratio (jailed/non-jailed mean wealth): 0.575 (1985) -> 0.538 (1990) -> 0.201 (1996); bootstrap CIs [0.342,0.876], [0.116,1.295], [0.110,0.327]. Ratios by tier at 1996: T1 0.276, T2 0.302, T3 0.164. The ratio, being scale-free, is unaffected by uniform inflation and by the undocumented composite's dollar level.

Descriptive contrast (NOT the answer, NOT causal - treated cells of 57/52/16/12/9):
| group | n jailed | ratio 85 -> 90 -> 96 |
|---|---|---|
| black male | 143 | 0.575 -> 0.538 -> 0.201 |
| black female | 16 | 0.155 -> 0.080 -> 0.081 |
| hipanic male | 52 | 0.256 -> 0.138 -> 0.542 |
| hipanic female | 9 | 0.007 -> 0.032 -> -0.012 |
| white male | 57 | 0.470 -> 0.292 -> 0.190 |
| white female | 12 | 0.622 -> 0.027 -> 0.050 |
The jail wealth deficit is NOT Black-specific (white and hispanic males show deficits too), but its TRAJECTORY differs: it deepens monotonically for Black and white males and does not for hispanic males (0.256->0.138->0.542), so the comparison-group evidence speaks to selection/differential labour markets rather than a Black-only effect. Black females (n=16 jailed) are non-inferential: means 1,662/1,988/3,494 vs non-jailed 10,741/24,731/43,101, ratio ~0.08 by 1996.

## 7. Limitations
ever_jailed is a timing- and duration-blind jail indicator for 1985-1994 with no treatment date, so 1985 wealth is a weak baseline (the window opens in 1985) and there is no pre-1985 wave to test parallel trends; no age, education, income, occupation or geography field exists; selection is real (jail rate 12-16% in the lowest baseline-wealth deciles vs 6.8% in the top, and the jailed group is already ~43% poorer in 1985); the five-component composite is undocumented; 39.7% of Black men sit at <=0 wealth producing massive ties; means are outlier-sensitive (excess kurtosis ~59-372). All findings are associations, not causal effects.
